{"id":"beats-968986","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"For a lightweight system (like Linear, GitHub Projects, or a simplified Jira setup), the golden rule is Parent states should be reflective, not directive.\n\nThe semantics work best when the Parent (Epic) acts as a container that summarizes the reality of its Children (Tasks), rather than a commander forcing state changes upon them.\n\n## Parent $\\rightarrow$ Child Sync (Downstream)\nScenario: An Epic is dragged into \"In Progress.\" \n\nShould children update? Recommendation: No.\n\n- Why: An Epic is rarely \"worked on\" directly; it is completed by finishing its parts. If you move an Epic to \"In Progress,\" it doesn't mean you have started working on every single sub-task.\n- The Trap: If dragging an Epic to \"In Progress\" auto-moves 20 tasks to \"In Progress,\" you destroy the accuracy of your board. You now have 20 tasks claiming to be active, but your team is likely only working on 2 or 3.\n- Exception: Closing/Canceling. If you move an Epic to \"Canceled\" or \"Archived,\" it should cascade down to cancel all open children, as the project itself is dead.\n\n\n## Child $\\rightarrow$ Parent Sync (Upstream)\nScenario: A child task is set to \"In Progress.\" \nShould the parent Epic update? Recommendation: Yes, conditionally.\n\nThis is the most helpful automation in lightweight systems. It keeps the high-level view accurate without manual admin work.\n- Rule: \"First-in, Last-out\"\n- Start: When the first child moves to \"In Progress,\" the Parent Epic automatically moves to \"In Progress.\"\n- Why: If a task has started, the project is by definition no longer just \"Planned.\"\n\n\n## Completion \u0026 Scope Creep\nScenario: All children are done. What happens to the Epic? What if we add more later?\n\n### Auto-Completion\nRecommendation: Prompt or Manual only (Avoid full auto-complete). While tempting, auto-completing the Epic when the last task finishes is often premature.\n\nThe Risk: You might finish the last engineering task, but the Epic still needs a PM review, documentation, or a release announcement.\nBetter Semantic: When the last child is done, trigger a notification or a visual \"check mark\" on the Epic, prompting the owner to close it. This invites a final \"sanity check.\"\n\n### Handling Scope Creep (Adding to a Done Epic)\nScenario: The Epic is \"Done,\" but you realize you forgot a bug, or the stakeholder adds a new requirement (a new child). \n\nRecommendation: Auto-Reopen the Parent."},"created_at":"2025-12-19T16:17:25.050136Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@kuy.io\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-6eac83","type":"CREATE","payload":{"kind":"BUG","title":"Show just status text, without icon in `beats show`","description":"Currently, when you run `beats show`, it displays an icon along with the status text. To improve readability and reduce clutter, we want to modify the command to display only the status text without any accompanying icon."},"created_at":"2025-12-19T16:19:16.439589Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@kuy.io\u003e"}
